## System Resource Safety

- Per-repository heavy-work limit: ONE build, full test run, recursive bulk copy, indexing/embedding, or dependency update at a time across that repository's sessions, worktrees, profiles, and subagents. Different repositories may run in parallel when host pressure permits. Global tool installation/update uses a separate shared maintenance slot. This takes precedence over parallel-delegation guidance.
- Default internal concurrency: 2 build/test workers; 1 browser/native E2E worker. Use invocation-scoped limits (Rust: CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=2 and --test-threads=2; Go: GOMAXPROCS=2, -p 2, -parallel 2).
- Check host pressure and existing work before starting. Defer new heavy work on memory warning/critical, known Heavy thermal pressure, CPU idle below 15% or 1-minute load at/above logical CPU count for 60 seconds, a new WindowServer watchdog event, or a user overload report. Missing telemetry is not proof of health.
- Resume at most one heavy job per repository only after 5 minutes of normal memory pressure, improved known thermal state, CPU idle at least 30%, and 1-minute load below 70% of logical CPU count. Do not immediately replay all jobs after reboot.
- Do not copy whole target/node_modules trees into parallel worktrees, including cp -cR. Reuse caches only through supported mechanisms without concurrent writers; otherwise build once with limited concurrency.
- Never launch a duplicate check because an existing one is slow. Await or inspect it; do not poll or retry in a tight loop. Perform required verification serially and report anything deferred as unverified.
- Use the repository-scoped admission controller and shared host-pressure checks when installed and verified; otherwise coordinate cooperatively. A controller only bounds participating jobs, so inspect existing work and defer if its ownership is unclear. Do not claim all sessions are automatically controlled.
- Do not kill another session, restart WindowServer/tccd/syspolicyd or memory workers, disable security checks, reset TCC, or reboot without explicit authorization.
- Honor any stricter workspace resource policy. Peer rollout requires that machine to be reachable and independently verified.

